[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says one issue, one fix queued. Its reports show three problems.
The queued parser fix closes the biggest one. The other two stay broken after it ships.

<Callout kind="warn">
Not one issue. Each problem now has its own ticket. Do not close them with #4471.
</Callout>

## What the reports show

<SplitTable ticket={4471} {tickets} />

The table reads every report merged into #4471 and recomputes on each build, so the counts
follow the desk as new reports come in.

## The three problems

**`parser`: day and month swapped.** The shared parser reads every date month first. Our tenants
write the day first, so 7 April comes out as 4 July. This is the bug #4471 describes, and the
fix queued for it.

**`hint`: the booking-v3 form asks for the wrong order.** booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its
date field. People type month first, as told, and the form reads the date day first. The dates
come out swapped too, but the parser fix does not touch them: the form hint has to change.
Only reports sent through the booking-v3 form have this cause.

**`shift`: a date moved by one day.** Some reports are not a swap at all. The date is one day
early or late: "Tuesday 6 October showed as Monday", "Client in Lisbon got Tuesday. Client in
Toronto got Monday." The desk marked these as one-day moves (`desk-notes.json`). A swap cannot
move a date by one day, so these reports have a cause of their own, probably time zones.
Nobody has looked yet.

## Why they were merged

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on subject line. All three problems
produce subjects like "Wrong date again", so they landed in one ticket. Intake's tag does not
help either: it guesses from the reporter's browser, and the table shows how often it guessed
a different cause.

## Lessons

- A big ticket can hold several bugs with the same symptom. Count by cause, not by subject.
- Check the outliers. Five reports that do not fit are a bug, not noise.

